Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-23
Subject: Drop virtual abstract on instantiated ogbase/cg base classes; bodies raise internalerror
License: expat

Several base classes in the assembler-output and codegen paths
(`tobjectdata` in `ogbase.pas`: `writereloc`, `writesymbol`,
`writestabs`, `writesymstabs`; `tcg` in the cg base: `getflagregister`;
the read-asm utilities: `parseparaloc`) declare core methods as
`virtual; abstract;`. Native FPC instantiates these classes anyway --
the abstract methods just happen never to be reached on those
instances at runtime, because real call sites go through concrete
backend subclasses. In native FPC the abstract slot is dead weight
the runtime never touches.

tp2cc emits each abstract Pascal method as a pure-virtual C++ method
(`= 0`). A C++ class with any pure-virtual is not instantiable at all,
so the source's "instantiate the base, just don't call this method"
pattern stops compiling.

Drop `abstract` from those declarations and supply each body with
`internalerror(...)`. The classes are now concretely instantiable
under both compilers; calling the method that was previously abstract
still fails loudly, just at the call site instead of the construction
site.

